    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [X] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version\n\n### Module(s)\n\nJump\n\n### Description\n\nHi, for the context, I am the author of https://github.com/ghostbuster91/nvim-next\r\n\r\nWhen trying to implement dot-repeat for my custom movements (`f`/`t`/etc) and failing miserably many times I finally came across your repository where it seems to work. Hats off to your vim-fu. \r\n\r\nHowever I noticed one bug - MiniJump consumes one character when using custom `f` motion with operator-pending and when the target does not exist. I tried fixing it myself but without luck.\r\nI even tried deleting the whole body of the function that is being passed to the expr but the character was still being consumed.\r\n\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.9.5\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nI changed `jump.lua` file so that it would invoke the setup when sourced:\r\n```diff\r\ndiff --git a/jump.lua b/jump.lua\r\nindex d5b0c9c..d7a1959 100644\r\n--- a/jump.lua\r\n+++ b/jump.lua\r\n@@ -488,4 +488,6 @@ function H.map(mode, key, rhs, opts)\r\n     vim.api.nvim_set_keymap(mode, key, rhs, opts)\r\n end\r\n \r\n+MiniJump.setup()\r\n+\r\n return MiniJump\r\n```\r\n\r\n1. `nvim -nu jump.lua`\r\n2. open any file with some text inside\r\n3. type `dfk` on a line where there is no `k` \r\n\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nNothing should happen when there is no target in the current line.\n\n### Actual behavior\n\nOne character is consumed. The same happens when we repeat action with `.`",
